                    DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION
              PROVIDES FIRST INTERACTIVE PARTICIPATION
                       IN PRESIDENTIAL DEBATES


PALO ALTO, Calif., October 3, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
will conduct the first live audience reaction surveys during the 
three U.S. presidential and vice presidential debates, scheduled for 
October 6, 9 and 16, 1996, through its pilot public service project, 
InPulse, a real-time, interactive, on-line polling service.  The 
project marks the first live audience participation in the debates 
and the first Web-based poll of this magnitude.  In addition, 
InPulse will enable the first on-line, real-time analysis of 
audience opinion during the debates.  Results will be available 
following the conclusion of each debate.

     "The InPulse project is yet another Internet first from 
Digital," said Dr. Brian Reid, director of Digital's Network Systems 
Laboratory in Palo Alto, Calif.  "As with our groundbreaking 
California election server in 1994, InPulse extends the capabilities 
and uses of the Internet into new domains, providing polling 
technology for future broadcast and Webcast programming.  Also, we 
think that innovative technology like InPulse, incorporating 
real-time results analysis, will help people feel they are more a 
part of the democratic process."

     To participate in the debates, members of the world Internet 
community will need a personal computer, Web browser and a 
television or radio.  InPulse participants are able to register at 
URL: http://debates96.election.digital.com by completing a 
pre-briefing debate questionnaire to identify basic demographics.  
They must register to take part in the survey project, but will not 
be required to provide their name or other identifying information.

     While watching or listening to the debates on a television or 
radio, registered users can respond to candidates' statements by 
"voting" via a Web-enabled PC.  To register their opinions, users 
click buttons located on their screen under each debater's name: 
strongly approve, approve, disapprove, strongly disapprove.  At any 
point during the debate, respondents can click the option that best 
represents their reaction to a candidate's statement.  There is no 
hard limit to the number of responses per user, but participants are 
encouraged to vote no more than once every 15 seconds.

     Digital will record, aggregate and display the voters' 
responses in graphic form both during and after each debate.  During 
the debate, users will be continuously updated on the number of 
active users and the number of total "votes" registered in the last 
second.  Following the debate, four levels of post-production 
results will be made available.  Results from levels one and two 
will start to be displayed within minutes of each debate's 
conclusion; results from levels three and four will be made 
available at the conclusion of all three debates.

     Level one results will graphically identify audience 
demographic data, including geography, political preference, age and 
gender.  Level two will complement this information with an audience 
response track - a timeline detailing approvals and disapprovals for 
each candidate for all 90 minutes of each debate.  Weighted values 
will be assigned to "strongly approves/disapproves" versus 
"approves/disapproves" to create an aggregate result.

     Following the conclusion of the debate series, level three of 
post-production results will synchronize the debate transcripts with 
the audience response tract, while level four will add a multimedia 
presentation superimposed on the audience response track.  
InPulse participants and interested Web surfers can go to 
http://debates96.election.digital.com, where Digital will display 
audience response tracks, demographic analyses and synchronized 
debate transcripts free of charge.

     InPulse is a not-for-profit research pilot developed by 
Digital's Research and Advanced Development (RAD) division of the 
Corporate Strategy and Technology (CST) Group.  The InPulse project 
uses four Alpha servers running Digital UNIX, an AlphaStation 500 
running Digital UNIX, a custom-built Web server, over 50MBs of 
available bandwidth and Digital's Palo Alto Internet gateway.  The 
only client requirement is a graphical Web browser that supports 
HTML tables.  For better timing and smoother performance, 
participants may use a Java-enabled browser, such as Netscape 3.0 or 
Microsoft Internet Explorer 3.0.

     Digital is promoting InPulse with newspaper partners through 
InfiNet Co., the leading provider of Internet services to the 
newspaper industry.  InfiNet, based in Norfolk, Virginia, is owned 
by Gannett, Knight-Ridder and Landmark Communications. It provides 
Internet publishing and access services to more than 60 newspapers.  
Other InPulse partners include InfiNet's Cool Site of the Day 
(www.coolsiteoftheday.com or cool.infi.net) and Live Online, a guide 
to Webcast events.

     "InPulse is a perfect partnership between our newspaper 
affiliates and Digital," said Bob Gilbert, president of InfiNet 
Publishing Services.  "Digital provides much of the world-class 
technical infrastructure for InfiNet's Publishing System, and our 
newspaper affiliates provide the most interesting and authoritative 
news coverage on the Internet today.

     "Together we will take the political pulse of a nation during 
the presidential debates."

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
