                 Digital's New Line Printers Deliver
      Low Cost, High Speed Printing for Industrial Environments


          ...Also Introduces New Ethernet Printer Server...


MAYNARD, Mass., October 9, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation's
new pedestal-mounted LGL4plus and LGL8plus line matrix printers 
provide optimized printing solutions for manufacturing and 
distribution customers who need high-quality, low cost, dependable 
printing.  These printers extend Digital's current line printer 
family of cabinet-based models, the LG04plus, LG08plus and LG12plus.

     Digital also announced the RapidPrint 500, a new Ethernet 
printer server, that allows users to share the same printer across 
networks from a variety of hosts and over different network 
protocols. 

     "These new printers provide industrial end users products 
specifically designed for their environments--rugged, easy-to-use, 
and with the lowest cost of ownership in the line printer world." 
said Rob Hannemann, general manager of Digital's Printing Systems 
Business Unit.  "Our new RapidPrint 500 micro server delivers 
mission-critical LAN printing for heterogeneous environments with an 
integrated networking solution."

LGL4plus and LGL8plus 

     Designed for mission-critical applications especially in 
manufacturing environments, the LGL4plus and LGL8plus line printers 
offer high-quality, low cost printing for bar coding, 
multi-part/continuous forms, production reports, invoices and bills 
of lading. The new printers support a variety of platforms including 
OpenVMS, UNIX, Windows 3.x, Windows 95 and Windows NT.  LGL4plus 
users can upgrade their printing performance with the installation 
of a faster LGL8plus series print mechanism.

RapidPrint 500 Multiprotocol Ethernet Micro Server 

     LAN connectivity for any printer, including the LGplus family, 
is enabled via the RapidPrint 500 multiprotocol Ethernet micro 
server.  A pocket sized, cable-end printer server, the RapidPrint 
500 allows users to share the same printers across the network from 
a variety of hosts and over different protocols. It protects users' 
investments by providing network connectivity for existing hosts, 
applications and printers, linking legacy systems with modern 
client/server environments.

     The RapidPrint 500 supports IPX, TCP/IP, NetBIOS/NetBEUI, LAT 
and AppleTalk network protocols and includes easy to use management 
software for all protocols running on any printer. RapidPrint 500 
runs without an AC converter on Digital's LGplus/LGLplus family of 
line matrix printers.

Pricing and Availability

     The LGL4plus and LGL8plus are available now through Digital-
authorized resellers and distributors. The LGL4plus is list priced 
at $5,295 and the LGL8plus model is list priced at $7,695. The 
RapidPrint 500, which will be available at the end of October, is 
list priced at $399. 

